{
  "narrative_body": "Marco Rubio flew to Rome, spent 90 minutes each with Giorgia Meloni and Antonio Tajani, and produced a diplomatic product called the 'Rome Coalition.' The day after, the American president told Corriere della Sera he was still considering withdrawing US troops from Italy and that Italy 'wasn't there when we needed them.'\n\nThat sequence tells the week's essential story. The Rubio visit was not a repair; it was managed co-existence. Italy held its position on the Strait of Hormuz — minesweepers only after a ceasefire and parliamentary authorization — and neither side broke the relationship. Mr Tajani announced the Rome Coalition on Hormuz food security and insisted 'nobody spoke of withdrawing troops.' The American president, the following morning, spoke of withdrawing troops. Carlo Calenda, the main opposition leader, said the meeting had 'produced nothing.'\n\nEven as the Rome talks produced their diplomatic framing, Ms Meloni was running a second track. Government sources — largely invisible in domestic press coverage that devoted 49 stories to the Rubio visit alone — confirm she attended the European Political Community Summit in Yerevan the same week, holding a bilateral with Volodymyr Zelensky and co-chairing a drug-coalition session with Emmanuel Macron. She also met Donald Tusk, the Polish prime minister, in Rome, received Libya's GNU prime minister Abdul Hamid Dabaiba, and visited Azerbaijan for talks with President Ilham Aliyev — whose country supplies Italy roughly 10 billion cubic meters of gas a year through the TAP pipeline, with capacity to double that. The European insurance policy was being reinforced in the same week the American relationship was under stress.\n\nThat insurance is becoming more expensive. French and British military chiefs, operating under the 'Willing' coalition framework, pressed their Italian counterparts to begin moving naval vessels toward Hormuz now — before any ceasefire — to be in position when one comes. Italian minesweepers need about 20 days to reach the strait from Italian ports. Mr Tajani floated pre-positioning them in the Red Sea alongside the Aspides mission, then immediately called it 'too early.' Guido Crosetto, the defense minister, repeated his condition: no deployment before a ceasefire, and only after Parliament authorizes it. Italy now faces the same demand from two directions — the US pushing it to act on Iran, its European partners pushing it to move ships. A joint parliamentary hearing on Hormuz is scheduled for May 13, where Mr Crosetto and Mr Tajani will testify. How they frame Italy's commitment will shape the next negotiating cycle with both Washington and Paris.\n\nAt the Alpine Corps Assembly in Genoa — 90,000 troops, a high-visibility domestic stage — Mr Crosetto put Italy's position in its sharpest form: 'The problem is not reconciling with the US but making the wars end.' The framing is consistent: he presents Italy's refusal to deploy before a ceasefire not as allied non-compliance but as principled conflict-resolution advocacy. The following day, the training ship Amerigo Vespucci sailed for North America ahead of the US independence 250th anniversary — a soft-power gesture toward a relationship that the week's politics had made visibly strained.\n\nThe domestic landscape shifted into new territory. Culture Minister Alessandro Giuli dismissed two senior ministry officials: Emanuele Merlino, head of the technical secretariat and a figure close to undersecretary Giovanbattista Fazzolari — Ms Meloni's key Palazzo Chigi operative — and Elena Proietti, a senior figure in the governing Fratelli d'Italia party in Umbria. Italian media read the firings as an intra-party power struggle, not a routine personnel change. Merlino's dismissal is linked to the blocking of a documentary on Giulio Regeni, the Italian student tortured and killed in Egypt, over which Mr Giuli had publicly committed to act. Francesco Lollobrigida, the FdI capodelegation minister, insisted the firings were within the minister's prerogative; opposition parties called it evidence of government chaos. The deeper significance is structural: previous friction in the coalition ran between the Lega party and FdI, or between Forza Italia and the rest. This runs inside FdI itself — Mr Giuli purging figures who answer to Ms Meloni's own Palazzo Chigi command structure.\n\nThe same week, deputy prime minister Matteo Salvini spent 25 minutes at the Russian pavilion at the Venice Biennale, listening to a traditional choir. Russia had been absent from the Biennale since 2022. Mr Giuli, who boycotted the opening in protest, publicly accused Mr Salvini of 'having drunk Russian disinformation' and called Russia's inclusion 'another victory for Russia.' Ms Meloni, according to La Repubblica, expressed private irritation; no public rebuke came. Mr Salvini framed his visit as support for 'free and courageous art.' The gesture was visible to Italy's allies in the same week Mr Rubio was in Rome asking Italy to act against Iranian pressure in the Gulf.\n\nThe coalition retains structural stability. No formal rupture has materialized, and Ms Meloni's pattern — private irritation, public containment — has kept the pieces together. But each week adds stressors without removing prior ones, and Italy is now 18 months from the 2027 electoral window, the period when coalition partners begin calculating their individual survival rather than the government's collective one.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Culture Ministry crisis: Giuli fires two senior FdI-linked staffers after Regeni documentary funding dispute",
      "summary": "Culture Minister Alessandro Giuli issued dismissal orders for two senior ministry officials — Emanuele Merlino, a figure close to undersecretary Fazzolari, and Elena Proietti — over failures linked to the blocking of a documentary on Giulio Regeni and an airport no-show. The firings were read across Italian media as a power struggle within Fratelli d'Italia. FdI capodelegation minister Lollobrigida publicly downplayed the episode as a routine personnel change; opposition parties PD and M5S called it evidence of government chaos."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Premier Meloni denounces AI-generated deepfake photo of herself in lingerie as political attack",
      "summary": "On May 5-6, Meloni published a post on Facebook reposting a deepfake AI-generated image showing her face on a body in underwear and called it a dangerous misuse of AI that could harm anyone. She referenced a prior legal case and said those responsible for spreading the image should be held accountable. Italian commentators debated her response, with some praising her handling and others noting the broader AI misinformation problem."
    },
    {
      "headline": "97th National Alpine Corps Assembly in Genoa draws 90,000 as Nave Vespucci departs for North America tour",
      "summary": "The three-day Alpine Corps (Alpini) national assembly concluded in Genoa on May 10 with a parade of 90,000 troops, attended by Defence Minister Crosetto and Chamber President Fontana. On May 9, the training ship Amerigo Vespucci departed Genoa for its North America 2026 campaign ahead of the US independence 250th anniversary. President Mattarella sent a message praising the Alpini. Crosetto used the occasion to address the Italy-US relationship and express hope for Italian naval ships to deploy to Hormuz after a ceasefire; he also defended the Alpini against harassment allegations."
    }
  ]
}

